# Ralph for OpenCode

A locally installable OpenCode plugin with durable session jobs, independent code and functional review, bounded review repairs, a read-only dashboard, and interruption recovery. Exact tested dependencies and platform results are in [compatibility](docs/opencode-compatibility.md).

This checkout is **not yet a complete feature workflow**: `/ralph-commands` has a tested proposal/approval/verification workflow, but the production adapter still lacks verified human approval provenance and stops before saving. `/ralph-orchestrator` now has a tested interview and explanation-approval workflow through the public adapter boundary. Production human approvals remain unsupported. PRD/graph generation and combined approval are tested with host-provided human provenance. Baseline preparation now creates an isolated feature branch/worktree and reports qualified validation through status. Single-issue TDD execution, concurrent dependency scheduling, serial integration and bounded repairs are available through the public command router; executor questions now wait in the main session while independent work continues, with same-attempt continuation and renewed approval for artifact revisions. The package demonstration seeds their execution handoff. Completing those phases and passing native Linux/Windows checks remain release blockers. See [package validation evidence](docs/package-validation.md).

## Build and install locally

Use Git and the Bun version in `.bun-version` (also pinned in `package.json`). From this source directory:

```sh
bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun run pack:local
```

Copy `opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz` to each target repository, then run there:

```sh
bun add ./opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz
bun -e 'console.log(require("node:url").pathToFileURL(require("node:fs").realpathSync("node_modules/opencode-ralph-plugin")).href)'
```

Add the generated, encoded URL to the repository's `opencode.json`, preserving any existing settings:

```json
{
  "plugins": ["REPLACE_WITH_GENERATED_PACKAGE_DIRECTORY_URL"]
}
```

Use the **entire** URL printed by the command. It points to the package directory, where OpenCode resolves `server.js` and `tui.js`; do not point it to `dist/plugin.js`. This handles Windows drives and spaces. The example uses single quotes around JavaScript for PowerShell and POSIX shells; native Windows results are pending CI. A relative tarball dependency is recommended; Bun 1.4.3 failed to install the tested percent-encoded absolute `file:` tarball URL with spaces. Each target must be a Git repository. The repository root need not have a commit just to load the plugin; feature/review operations need committed inputs.

Start OpenCode **2.0.29** in that repository (see compatibility for the local binary path). Restart OpenCode after changing plugin configuration. No registry publication is required or performed by these scripts.

## Commands and operation

- `/ralph-commands` explores lifecycle commands in a read-only session, presents the exact revision/hash, and requires a correlated human approval before saving `.ralph/commands.json`. Production approval currently reports unsupported; a host adapter with verified input provenance is required to complete setup. Each execution also requires host permission.

- `/ralph-orchestrator [feature]` requires verified project commands for the current configuration and host. It collects missing feature input, explores the repository in a read-only interview session, asks recommended questions in the main session, and persists the conversation. Reply `approve <revision>` to the presented explanation, or describe revisions. Approved explanations continue through PRD/graph generation and combined execution approval. Execution approval presents the starting SHA and runs baseline preflight when the checkout is clean; setup/check failures block, and check timeouts remain visible as run-local omissions. A passed baseline automatically schedules dependency-ready issues with two executor slots by default and one integration queue. The production adapter reports unavailable human-input provenance and leaves the request pending.

- `/ralph-status [run-id]` reads durable status and the dashboard URL. With no argument it selects the latest run.
- `/ralph-pause [run-id]` records pause intent and freezes new dispatch while active work settles.
- `/ralph-resume [run-id]` records explicit resume intent after pause/recovery; preserved attempts and revisions remain in force. Blocked recovery requires resolving its disclosed facts first.
- `/ralph-stop [run-id]` cancels correlated sessions and cleans up owned processes. Stale completions cannot advance the run.

The dashboard URL includes an access token in its fragment. Open it locally; treat it as private. The listener uses loopback with an automatically allocated port, so independent repositories can run together. The browser is read-only; commands and approvals belong in OpenCode. State and write locks live under the repository's canonical Git common directory, `ralph/runs`, and are shared by linked worktrees. Independent repositories use independent stores. The plugin stays attached to the OpenCode server lifecycle; shutdown stops owned work and the dashboard. Restart reconciles facts and waits for explicit resume.

The v2 package ships server and TUI entry points. Slash commands execute directly, emit a status notification, and queue their exact output as a synthetic inbox item without waking the model. The TUI displays Ralph status notifications; the dashboard and persisted store remain authoritative.

The public `adapter`, `store`, `commands`, `types`, `review/*`, `reporting/completion` and `process` exports support typed host integration. `createCommandRouter(...).scheduleIssues(runId)` schedules the approved DAG; `executeIssue(runId, issueId)` executes/integrates one approved issue with at most three attempts and retained failure evidence; `answerExecutorQuestion(runId, issueId, response)` validates a host-provided correlated human response; `continuePlanning(runId)` presents and continues a pending execution approval; `runJob` dispatches a correlated session; `reviewFeature` coordinates the two reviewers and final report. `process` exposes ownership tracking/cleanup for approved command owners, not command approval. Tests and fixture code are excluded from runtime exports and the tarball.

Code and functional reviewers load their bundled `skills/code-review` and `skills/functional-review` resources. Code-review options accept a `{ providerID, modelID }` model; functional review accepts its own model through `functionalOptions`. Executor jobs carry the same optional model field. Model/tool availability and OpenCode permissions remain host-owned; no permission is auto-approved. Command configuration validates lifecycle ordering, platform coverage (or explicit unsupported reasons), paths, timeouts, argument arrays, explicit shells and environment references. Interview role settings are read from optional `.ralph/project.json`; other planning phases remain pending.

## Interview settings

Project replacements resolve relative to the workspace root. Omit `skillPaths.interview`
to use the original bundled grill-me guidance. Omit `models.interview` to capture the main
session's selected model; an unavailable selection produces an actionable error.

```json
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "models": { "interview": { "providerID": "your-provider", "modelID": "your-model" } },
  "skillPaths": { "interview": "skills/my-interview.md" },
  "concurrency": 2,
  "timeoutMs": 300000,
  "agentTimeoutMs": 1800000
}
```

Runs record model selections, skill paths and content hashes, the verified command hash,
and timeout/concurrency defaults. Agent timeouts interrupt planning and retain the lock
and evidence. Human answers do not time out. `agentTimeoutMs` separately controls executor and merger deadlines (default thirty minutes), and is recorded on the run and each issue attempt. One canonical repository lock includes
planning; explanation approval retains it for the next planning slice. Interrupted locks
require reconciliation; this slice does not release them or automatically restart planning.

## Concurrent execution resources

Ready issues are selected in approved graph order. A submission waits in the integration
queue; children start only after all parents integrate, including qualified integration
with omitted checks. `/ralph-status` and durable events expose ready, pending, running,
integration_queued, integrated, failed and blocked states plus queue order. The executor
slot ends after submission cleanup; merger/validation work uses a separate serial queue.

`/ralph-commands` may propose resource allocation in the exact approved configuration:

```json
{
  "resources": {
    "RALPH_PORT": "port",
    "RALPH_NAMESPACE": "namespace",
    "RALPH_DIRECTORY": "directory"
  },
  "exclusiveResources": ["shared-test-database"]
}
```

Command `env` mappings reference these source names, for example
`{"PORT":"RALPH_PORT","APP_NAMESPACE":"RALPH_NAMESPACE","APP_DATA":"RALPH_DIRECTORY"}`.
Ports, namespaces and directories are allocated per lifecycle/job. Executor prompts
receive the allocation; candidates receive fresh allocations. The runner executes only
approved commands, using main-session permission before an executor session exists.
Shared resource names serialize use within the repository. Startup without allocated
ports also serializes use. Missing required references block setup; commands are never
invented or rewritten to obtain isolation. Owned cleanup releases only that lifecycle's
resources; cleanup failures retain ownership and diagnostics.

## Executor questions

An executor may return `{"kind":"needs_input","question":"...","recommendedAnswer":"...","affectedRequirement":"..."}`. Ralph persists the question on its run/issue/attempt and publishes it through the main-session adapter. The issue and descendants wait while independent issues continue. Human waiting consumes no executor capacity, agent deadline or retry. A correlated clarification resumes the same worktree and attempt with a new job identity and the remaining active-time budget.

Hosts answer through the adapter's authenticated `UserInputResult` contract. The public router also accepts a later response via `answerExecutorQuestion`, followed by `scheduleIssues` for clarification or `continuePlanning` for revision approval. Stale requests, changed revisions, duplicate messages and unrelated sessions are rejected. The production adapter currently leaves human input pending because verified provenance is unavailable.

For scope changes, a correlated user answer can carry JSON `{"kind":"revise_scope","prd":"complete PRD text","graph":<complete graph>,"files":<issue-path to Markdown map>}`. Ralph compares content, validates and publishes a new draft, presents affected work and preserved integrated evidence, and requires the normal combined execution approval. Adapter-delivered answers present that gate automatically. Revised artifacts cannot execute under the previous approval. Answers and approvals have no browser mutation endpoint.

## Validation semantics

Three **total issue attempts** include the first submission and two retries; an expected failing TDD red test does not consume an attempt. Three **total review rounds** apply to the final dual review; each generated repair task has its own three-attempt limit. Functional infrastructure retries have a separate three-attempt budget. Issue repair limits are enforced through the public single-issue entrypoint; the dependency scheduler preserves independent progress when an issue exhausts its budget.

The intended validation policy skips only timed-out validation checks for the current run, never setup/readiness timeouts, permission denials, cancellations or completed failures. Those omissions must remain in the report and must not rewrite approved commands. The general timeout/skip executor and clean-checkout baseline are pending earlier slices; the demo supplies skipped evidence explicitly. A baseline must eventually pass on an isolated clean checkout before feature implementation. Retain failed worktrees and their evidence for diagnosis; clean successful worktrees only after validated integration. Recovery does not silently discard retained resources.

A completed agent job is not proof of a verified feature. Reports distinguish `verified`, `incomplete-validation` (including skipped checks), `unverified` (missing review evidence) and `unresolved` (blocking issues/findings). Inspect the report's commit, checks, unavailable review and cleanup/resource fields. No workflow script publishes, pushes, creates a PR or merges into a target branch.

## Reproduce the package demonstration

```sh
bun test tests/integration/15-package.test.ts
bun run test:package
bun test tests/e2e/full-workflow.test.ts
bun run test:browser
bun run test:smoke
```

The package probe builds a clean distribution, packs it, installs it into two committed temporary repositories with spaces in their paths, and loads the installed plugin. It checks bundled skills/dashboard assets, separate stores and dashboard data, concurrent ports, argv preservation, local readiness, child cleanup and pause/resume. The review demo commits a tiny addition feature, runs its test, seeds the absent execution handoff, and dispatches independent reviewer sessions through the fake SDK HTTP boundary. Reports are `verified` for the passing-evidence fixture and `incomplete-validation` for the skipped-evidence fixture. These are deterministic fixture verdicts, not live-agent evidence. Temporary repositories are removed; `test:package` prints platform, runtime, outcomes and blockers. Set `PACKAGE_EVIDENCE_PATH` to retain JSON evidence.

For the real installed OpenCode server probe, enable `OPENCODE_SMOKE=1` (PowerShell: `$env:OPENCODE_SMOKE='1'`) before `bun run test:smoke`. It uses a local deterministic model. The separate credentialed session probe requires `OPENCODE_LIVE_SMOKE=1`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_URL` and `OPENCODE_SMOKE_MODEL=provider/model`; without them it reports SKIPPED. Neither probe covers the absent setup-to-report workflow. A v2 server also requires authentication: set `OPENCODE_SMOKE_PASSWORD` for the live probe. Credentials remain in the configured server; do not copy them into fixtures or package assets.

See [troubleshooting](docs/troubleshooting.md). CI runs the required suites on macOS, Ubuntu and native Windows and retains per-platform package evidence. A green fixture demo does not remove the missing-phase or unrun-platform blockers.
# OpenCode compatibility

## Supported combination

This package targets **OpenCode 2.0.29**. Its server/TUI definitions and typed adapter use the v2 API; v1 hosts are no longer supported. Version 0.2.0 replaces the earlier 1.19.0 integration.

| Component | Exact version |
| --- | --- |
| `@opencode/cli` (development/test CLI) | 2.0.29 |
| `@opencode/plugin`, `@opencode/client`, `@opencode/schema` | 2.0.29 |
| Bun | 1.4.3 |
| TypeScript / Biome | 5.9.3 / 2.4.6 |

The real-server smoke probe builds, packs, installs and loads the package in an isolated Git repository. On macOS arm64 it exercises completed sessions, direct commands without model calls, event subscription, denied shell execution, linked-worktree observation restrictions, persistence, restart, explicit resume, interruption and status RPC. The deterministic local model proves the host boundary, not credentialed external-provider behavior. Native Linux/Windows results remain pending CI.

## Installation and entry points

Follow [README](../README.md). Configure `plugins` with the installed **package directory**, preferably a canonical, encoded file URL. Do not use the old `plugin` entry pointing to `dist/plugin.js`. OpenCode resolves `server.js` and `tui.js` from that directory; both have default definitions with `id: 'ralph'` and `setup(context)`. Package imports also expose `/server` and `/tui`.

Tests use `node_modules/@opencode/cli/bin/opencode.exe`, including on macOS/Linux. The manifest trusts this pinned CLI's postinstall script. Version changes require new runtime evidence, not just a dependency bump.

V2 activates external plugins asynchronously. Wait for command registration before dispatching external jobs; otherwise initial recovery may encounter newly recorded job intent. Local configuration changes require host activation/reload to complete.

Command registration can be visible before `plugin.list` publishes the active plugin. The installed-package probe also waits for that inventory entry before checking server/TUI features. This preserves the supported v2 mapping described in the [official plugin documentation](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/build/plugins/); the ticket's proposed `@opencode-ai/plugin` import belongs to v1.

## Session adapter

`src/opencode/adapter.ts` accepts a narrow `AdapterHost`; `createAdapterClient` supplies it over the real v2 client and Ralph RPC. The plugin supplies the host's native session domain. V2 calls use flattened options and throw typed/transport failures rather than returning v1 `{ data, error, response }` envelopes.

```ts
const client = createAdapterClient(url, undefined, {
  Authorization: `Basic ${btoa('opencode:' + password)}`,
});
const session = await client.session.create({
  location: { directory }, title,
  model: { providerID, id: modelID },
});
const receipt = await client.session.prompt({ sessionID: session.id, text });
await client.session.wait({ sessionID: session.id });
const history = await client.session.context({ sessionID: session.id });
await client.session.interrupt({ sessionID: session.id });
await client.session.command({ sessionID: session.id, name: 'ralph-status', text: runId });
const commands = await client.command.list({ location: { directory } });
for await (const event of client.event.subscribe({ signal })) { /* v2 event.data */ }
```

Job intent is durable before session creation; the returned session ID is durable before prompting. Workspace selection occurs at session creation; subsequent calls target that session ID. Model fields map from Ralph's `{ providerID, modelID }` contract to v2 `{ providerID, id }`.

Prompt acknowledgment is not completion. The adapter waits, finds the delivered user message with the receipt's ID, and checks the subsequent turn. Success requires completed assistant text, `finish: 'stop'`, a successful idle record and no failed tools or session errors. Unexpected subsequent input makes correlation uncertain and returns missing output. Earlier failed/denied tools, including caught code-mode failures in tool metadata, cannot be erased by later assistant success.

Cancellation is recorded before interruption. Journals retain the existing `session.abort` operation kind for compatibility with persisted state; v2 uses `session.interrupt` underneath. A late-created cancelled session is interrupted without prompting. Stale results cannot update superseded attempts.

## Commands, observations and notifications

Commands register with `context.command.transform`; tools register with `context.tool.transform`. Status/pause/resume/stop execute directly. The exact command output is queued as a synthetic inbox item with `resume: false`, and a status event notifies the TUI without waking the model. Pending output can be inspected with `client.session.inbox.list`; it is consumed on the next session execution. Status never depends on generated assistant text.

V2 does not expose the plugin tool catalog as a public client endpoint. `src/opencode/bridge.ts` defines location-scoped Ralph RPC for tool discovery, observation preparation and status notification. Review sessions expose only `ralph_review_*` aliases of permitted observation tools. Aliases retain each original tool's permission action and execution function. Context filtering hides mutation tools and code-mode `execute`; the execution hook also rejects unapproved calls. Normal sessions retain their original tools and hide the observation aliases. Host denials are never changed to allows and no permissions are automatically approved.

The TUI entry listens for `rpc.ralph.status` in its location and shows an informational toast; cleanup unsubscribes. Server-only consumers can subscribe to status RPC events and use the durable status/dashboard. Interactive TUI rendering has unit contract coverage; native interactive/platform validation remains pending.

## Recovery and remaining limits

The canonical Git common directory owns `<git-common-dir>/ralph/runs`. Its fsynced JSONL journal remains authoritative; snapshots use atomic rename and writers use ownership-verified locks. Linked worktrees share state. Multiple v2 locations in one server recover that state once and only the last location's cleanup stops shared work. Restart still requires explicit resume and never resets attempts or review budgets. No new state schema or migration is needed for existing journals.

V2 exposes session forms, but Ralph's human-input/approval provenance workflow is still absent. `requestUserInput` remains explicitly unsupported; neither assistant text nor API-created user messages establish human approval. The command setup workflow now validates correlated host-supplied approvals and lifecycle execution in fixtures, with production execution permissions delegated to `ctx.permission.assert`. Production command approval, interview/PRD approval, baseline preparation and issue scheduling/execution remain unfinished. See [README](../README.md) and [open questions](wiki/open-questions.md).

## Reproduce

```sh
bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun run test
OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun run test:smoke
bun run test:package
```

On PowerShell set `$env:OPENCODE_SMOKE='1'` before running the smoke suite. The local probe uses an isolated test-only Basic-auth password. The separate live probe requires `OPENCODE_LIVE_SMOKE=1`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_URL`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_MODEL=provider/model` and the server's `OPENCODE_SMOKE_PASSWORD`; without its opt-in/endpoint/model it is skipped. Do not put credentials in package resources or fixture data.

## Issue 1 verification — 2026-10-14

- macOS arm64, Bun 1.4.3: typecheck and lint passed; all 19 issue-1 integration tests passed.
- `OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun test tests/smoke/opencode.test.ts`: **2 passed, 1 skipped**. The fresh-source install uses only the manifest/lockfile and an empty package cache. The installed-package probe builds, packs and loads Ralph in real OpenCode 2.0.29, correlates its local model response through the public router/adapter, preserves permission denial, observes events and verifies status after host restart.
- Red/green evidence: the empty-cache install failed with `ENOENT extracting tarball from opencode-ralph-plugin` before removing the accidental self-dependency from the manifest and regenerating the lockfile. It then passed. A warm cache had concealed the missing archive. Package installation must not depend on a previously built copy of itself.
- The real probe initially failed on an unpublished plugin inventory entry; the probe now waits for active inventory independently of command registration, retaining its TUI assertion.
- Fresh-install checks are gated because they download dependencies; the deterministic issue-1 suite requires neither network model access nor paid calls. Credentialed external-provider execution was skipped. Linux and Windows have CI jobs but were not run here; interactive TUI and human-response provenance remain unverified/unsupported as described above.
# Package validation — 2026-10-14

This page includes historical slice-15 evidence; current OpenCode 2.0.29 mapping and validation are in [compatibility](opencode-compatibility.md). This is implementation evidence for slice 15, not a release certification. The source currently lacks setup/interview/PRD/graph approval, baseline preparation, issue scheduling/execution and the active-run owner from slices 2–9. Those phases cannot be demonstrated honestly and remain release blockers. See [installation and demo](../README.md).

## Behavioral changes and red/green evidence

- A new regression failed on the manifest's absolute developer-local self-dependency. Removing that dependency and updating `bun.lock` made it pass.
- The real installed-server restart exposed a reentrant startup deadlock: initialization awaited an SDK toast into the initializing host. A regression with a held notification failed before the fix and passed after notifications were deferred until initialization returns. Durable recovery/status remains immediately available; disposal cancels pending notifications.
- The package integration tracer initially failed before the isolated packaging harness existed. It then installed and loaded real tarballs in two consumers with separate durable state/dashboard listeners.
- The installed ownership probe failed because `/process` was not exported. Exposing the existing ownership API allowed the installed wrapper, argv, readiness and cleanup checks to pass.
- The review demo initially rejected a fixture response missing the required code-review role identity. Correcting the fixture produced the expected `verified` and `incomplete-validation` reports; production result validation was preserved.
- Bun 1.4.3 failed to install a percent-encoded absolute tarball URL with spaces. The portable fixture and docs now use a copied tarball with a relative dependency. Plugin loading still correctly uses encoded file URLs.
- A stale `dist/runtime-token-canary.log` is removed by the clean build and must be absent from installed contents. Allowlisted package roots exclude source tests, logs, fixtures, credentials and developer configuration. Dashboard JS is fetched from the installed listener and role skills are consumed by actual reviewer dispatch.

Manifest/docs/CI changes are scaffolding; the executable packaging/installed workflow probes cover their observable behavior. No synthetic tests mirror YAML or documentation text.

## Historical 1.19.0 check results

Exact versions: Bun 1.4.3 (`c6da4a4d3`), OpenCode CLI/plugin/SDK 1.19.0, TypeScript 5.9.3, Biome 2.4.6, Playwright 1.56.1. Local platform: macOS arm64. See [compatibility mapping](opencode-compatibility.md).

| Check | Local result |
| --- | --- |
| `bun run typecheck` / `bun run lint` | PASS |
| `bun run test` (30-second allowance per integration test) | PASS; final count recorded below |
| `bun test tests/integration/15-package.test.ts` | PASS; manifest, two installed consumers, held-notification startup regression |
| `bun run build` / `bun run pack:local` | PASS; clean TypeScript/dashboard build and local archive |
| `bun run test:browser` | PASS: all 5 dashboard/report Chromium tests |
| `OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun run test:smoke` | PASS: 2 passed, 1 skipped, 21 assertions; real OpenCode/local-model package probe plus deterministic two-consumer smoke |
| `bun test tests/e2e/full-workflow.test.ts` | PASS for available review/report demo; complete setup-to-report test explicitly SKIPPED |
| `bun run test:package` | PASS; fixture outcomes `verified` and `incomplete-validation` |
| Credentialed external-model session | SKIPPED: no explicit endpoint/model/opt-in |
| Native Linux / Windows | NOT RUN locally; required CI results pending |

The real installed-server probe passes session responses, command registration, events, permission denial, restart/persisted status, explicit resume, abort and toast. Its first startup race was fixture-induced: it recorded intent before lazy plugin initialization. The probe now waits for registration first. Its restart failure exposed the production toast deadlock fixed above. Event collection and owned server shutdown now have bounded cleanup; request deadlines avoid stalled smoke runs.

Native Linux and Windows results require their CI jobs. Credentialed session smoke does not cover the missing product phases. Live owned-service lifecycle inside a native OpenCode host is also not established by the Bun-based process fixture.

## Demonstrated scenario and limits

`bun run test:package` builds, packs and installs in two committed repositories with spaces. It loads the installed plugin and exported adapter/store/router/process modules, fetches dashboard HTML/JS, checks separate durable stores and data, runs literal argv through an owned app process, observes readiness and verifies descendant shutdown. Both dashboards coexist on separate allocated ports.

The demo commits a small feature and runs a real behavior test, then explicitly seeds execution evidence from the absent predecessor. Independent reviewer jobs consume bundled skills through the controllable HTTP adapter. One repository yields a fixture `verified` report; the other includes a synthetic timeout omission and yields `incomplete-validation`. The demo does not simulate a real hung check or prove command approval, graph scheduling, TDD execution or an active-run lock. Those belong to missing predecessor slices. The store's per-repository writer locks are exercised by actual writes; that is distinct from a production active-feature lock.

The full setup-to-report test is explicitly SKIPPED with its blocker. The optional credentialed test probes only an external-model session and remains separately gated. Fake/local model success is not live compatibility. The CI matrix includes all browser suites and packaged tests on macOS, Ubuntu and native Windows and uploads per-platform JSON evidence. Any missing/failed platform requirement remains a release blocker.

No publication, push, PR creation or target-branch merge occurs. Local feature fixture commits are retained only for the duration of the test. Failed runtime worktrees remain governed by the existing recovery/review contracts; package probes clean their own temporary repositories.

The first broad run hit Bun's default five-second limit in an existing four-fixture review test while other checks were running. `bun run test` now gives each integration test 30 seconds for native Git fixtures; the rerun passed 71 tests, skipped one gated agent-browser check, and failed none (375 assertions). Product retry/timeout policy was unchanged.
name: Compatibility
on: [push, pull_request, workflow_dispatch]
jobs:
  plugin:
    strategy:
      fail-fast: false
      matrix:
        os: [macos-latest, ubuntu-latest, windows-latest]
    runs-on: ${{ matrix.os }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@v2
        with:
          bun-version-file: .bun-version
      - run: bun install --frozen-lockfile
      - run: bun run typecheck
      - run: bun run lint
      - run: bun run test
      - run: bun run build
      - run: bunx --no-install playwright install --with-deps chromium
      - run: bun run test:browser
      - run: bun run test:smoke
        env:
          OPENCODE_SMOKE: '1'
      - run: bun test tests/e2e/full-workflow.test.ts
      - run: bun run test:package
        env:
          PACKAGE_EVIDENCE_PATH: package-evidence.json
      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        if: always()
        with:
          name: package-evidence-${{ matrix.os }}
          path: package-evidence.json
          if-no-files-found: warn
### Distribution — in scope

- ✅ Reusable installable OpenCode plugin package.
- ✅ macOS, Linux, and native Windows support.
- ✅ One active feature workflow per repository, including its linked worktrees.

### Out of scope

- ❌ Automatic target-branch merge, push, deployment, or pull request creation.
- ❌ GitHub issue synchronization or another external issue tracker.
- ❌ Browser-based answers, approvals, or execution controls.
- ❌ Execution continuing independently after OpenCode exits.
- ❌ Multiple concurrent feature workflows in one repository.
- ❌ Formal ASD-STE100 certification or numerical compliance scoring.
- ❌ Automatic additions or changes to approved commands during feature execution.
- ❌ Automatic success claims when validation or functional review is incomplete.

## 5. User Stories

1. **As a developer, I want project checks discovered once, so that every issue uses consistent validation.** For example, Ralph proposes dependency installation, type checking, tests, and local app startup before saving an approved JSON file.
2. **As a feature owner, I want an interview before implementation, so that ambiguous requirements become explicit.** For example, a password-reset feature specifies token expiry and user-visible error behavior before PRD approval.
3. **As a developer, I want to approve the PRD and issue graph together, so that I can verify scope and execution order before code changes begin.**
4. **As a developer, I want independent issues to execute concurrently, so that parallel work reduces completion time.** If C depends on A and B, A and B can run together, but C waits until both are integrated and checked.
5. **As a maintainer, I want TDD and isolated merge validation, so that each accepted issue has meaningful behavior tests and does not break the integrated feature.**
6. **As a developer, I want bounded autonomous retries, so that routine failures do not require immediate supervision.** A stuck executor retries within three total attempts; a hung validation command is skipped for that feature run and disclosed.
7. **As a feature owner, I want independent code and functional review, so that a passing test command is not the only evidence of correctness.** The functional reviewer records results against PRD acceptance criteria.
8. **As a developer, I want a dashboard and recoverable state, so that I can understand progress and resume after interruption.** Reopening OpenCode shows reconciled run status and waits for my resume instruction.
## 11. Success Criteria and Acceptance Tests

### MVP acceptance

- ✅ Install the package in more than one repository with independent project configuration and state.
- ✅ Prevent feature interviews until approved command setup verifies successfully.
- ✅ Complete interview, explanation approval, PRD, new-session issue generation, and execution approval in OpenCode.
- ✅ Reject invalid dependency graphs before starting work.
- ✅ Run two independent issues concurrently; start a dependent issue only after all prerequisites integrate and pass non-skipped checks.
- ✅ Demonstrate behavior-focused TDD evidence for execution and repair jobs, with documented valid exceptions.
- ✅ Keep the integration branch unchanged when candidate validation fails.
- ✅ Enforce three total issue attempts and three total final review rounds.
- ✅ Skip timed-out validation checks for the current run only and disclose them even when every check is skipped.
- ✅ Never skip hung setup commands under the validation skip policy.
- ✅ Preserve failed worktrees and remove successful issue worktrees after validated integration.
- ✅ Derive functional review from acceptance criteria and distinguish unavailable verification from successful tests.
- ✅ Resume after interruption without duplicate integration or lost approval context.
- ✅ Leave the feature branch intact without pushing or modifying the target branch.
- ✅ Pass platform coverage on macOS, Linux, and native Windows.

### Required verification scenarios

1. Happy path: setup, interview, approved graph, parallel issues, sequential integrations, two passing reviewers, retained feature branch.
2. Diamond dependency graph: A unlocks B and C; D waits for both integrated commits.
3. Concurrent submissions: each candidate is tested against the latest integration state, never a stale predecessor.
4. Failure loop: initial submission and two repairs fail; block descendants and continue unrelated issues.
5. TDD red-stage failure does not consume an issue attempt.
6. Hung agent consumes an attempt; cancelled or late session output cannot advance state.
7. Timed-out check is killed and skipped for later work in that run; nonzero exits are not skipped; the next run retries the check.
8. All checks skipped: execution continues, but final status and report identify missing validation.
9. Hung setup: retry within the applicable budget and block affected work rather than marking it ready.
10. Review rounds: repair tasks integrate before both reviewers inspect the new stable commit; unresolved round-three findings stop automatic repairs.
11. Functional review unavailable: after three attempts, report unverified completion with the exact blocker.
12. Recovery during integration: reconcile a commit already advanced before the state write; do not merge it twice.
13. Permission denial, missing tool, unavailable test credentials, and failed cleanup produce visible, accurately classified results.
14. Dashboard rejects mutation requests and unauthorized access; logs distinguish skipped, failed, and passed checks.
15. Native Windows: paths with spaces, shell selection, app readiness, and child-process cleanup work correctly.

Measure phase durations, attempts, integration results, and verification coverage locally. No external telemetry is required for MVP. Prefer deterministic fake-agent tests for orchestration limits, plus small real OpenCode end-to-end tests for SDK integration.

## 12. Implementation Phases

Estimates assume one experienced engineer with agent assistance. They are planning ranges, not delivery commitments.

| Phase | Goal and deliverables | Validation | Estimate |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1. Integration foundation | ✅ Pin supported versions; verify command registration, sessions, permission behavior, worktree directory routing, persistence, and process lifecycle; package skeleton | Minimal installed plugin works on all three platforms; session and permission behavior demonstrated | 1–2 weeks |
| 2. Setup and planning | ✅ Command discovery/approval/verification; bundled skills; interview; PRD; new-session issue planning; graph validation and approval | Run a sample feature through approved local issues; reject invalid configuration and stale approvals | 1–2 weeks |
| 3. Execution engine | ✅ Scheduler; TDD job contract; worktrees; merger; checks; retry/skip policies; environment isolation; recovery; dashboard | Deterministic graph/failure tests and real Git integration tests pass, including Windows | 2–3 weeks |
| 4. Review and release | ✅ Both reviewers; repair tasks; round limits; qualified completion reports; documentation; installation and end-to-end tests | Full sample feature, timeout, and crash-recovery scenarios pass; reusable package installs cleanly | 1–2 weeks |

Expected total: approximately 5–9 weeks, with OpenCode API compatibility and native Windows process handling as the main schedule uncertainties.
