# Ralph for OpenCode

A locally installable OpenCode plugin with durable session jobs, independent code and functional review, bounded review repairs, a read-only dashboard, and interruption recovery. Exact tested dependencies and platform results are in [compatibility](docs/opencode-compatibility.md).

This checkout is **not yet a complete feature workflow**: `/ralph-commands` has a tested proposal/approval/verification workflow, but the production adapter still lacks verified human approval provenance and stops before saving. `/ralph-orchestrator` now has a tested interview and explanation-approval workflow through the public adapter boundary. Production human approvals remain unsupported. PRD/graph generation and combined approval are tested with host-provided human provenance. Baseline preparation now creates an isolated feature branch/worktree and reports qualified validation through status. Single-issue TDD execution, concurrent dependency scheduling, serial integration and bounded repairs are available through the public command router; executor questions now wait in the main session while independent work continues, with same-attempt continuation and renewed approval for artifact revisions. The package demonstration seeds their execution handoff. Completing those phases and passing native Linux/Windows checks remain release blockers. See [package validation evidence](docs/package-validation.md).

## Build and install locally

Use Git and the Bun version in `.bun-version` (also pinned in `package.json`). From this source directory:

```sh
bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun run pack:local
```

Copy `opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz` to each target repository, then run there:

```sh
bun add ./opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz
bun -e 'console.log(require("node:url").pathToFileURL(require("node:fs").realpathSync("node_modules/opencode-ralph-plugin")).href)'
```

Add the generated, encoded URL to the repository's `opencode.json`, preserving any existing settings:

```json
{
  "plugins": ["REPLACE_WITH_GENERATED_PACKAGE_DIRECTORY_URL"]
}
```

Use the **entire** URL printed by the command. It points to the package directory, where OpenCode resolves `server.js` and `tui.js`; do not point it to `dist/plugin.js`. This handles Windows drives and spaces. The example uses single quotes around JavaScript for PowerShell and POSIX shells; native Windows results are pending CI. A relative tarball dependency is recommended; Bun 1.4.3 failed to install the tested percent-encoded absolute `file:` tarball URL with spaces. Each target must be a Git repository. The repository root need not have a commit just to load the plugin; feature/review operations need committed inputs.

Start OpenCode **2.0.29** in that repository (see compatibility for the local binary path). Restart OpenCode after changing plugin configuration. No registry publication is required or performed by these scripts.

## Commands and operation

- `/ralph-commands` explores lifecycle commands in a read-only session, presents the exact revision/hash, and requires a correlated human approval before saving `.ralph/commands.json`. Production approval currently reports unsupported; a host adapter with verified input provenance is required to complete setup. Each execution also requires host permission.

- `/ralph-orchestrator [feature]` requires verified project commands for the current configuration and host. It collects missing feature input, explores the repository in a read-only interview session, asks recommended questions in the main session, and persists the conversation. Reply `approve <revision>` to the presented explanation, or describe revisions. Approved explanations continue through PRD/graph generation and combined execution approval. Execution approval presents the starting SHA and runs baseline preflight when the checkout is clean; setup/check failures block, and check timeouts remain visible as run-local omissions. A passed baseline automatically schedules dependency-ready issues with two executor slots by default and one integration queue. The production adapter reports unavailable human-input provenance and leaves the request pending.

- `/ralph-status [run-id]` reads durable status and the dashboard URL. With no argument it selects the latest run.
- `/ralph-pause [run-id]` records pause intent and freezes new dispatch while active work settles.
- `/ralph-resume [run-id]` records explicit resume intent after pause/recovery; preserved attempts and revisions remain in force. Blocked recovery requires resolving its disclosed facts first.
- `/ralph-stop [run-id]` cancels correlated sessions and cleans up owned processes. Stale completions cannot advance the run.

The dashboard URL includes an access token in its fragment. Open it locally; treat it as private. The listener uses loopback with an automatically allocated port, so independent repositories can run together. The browser is read-only; commands and approvals belong in OpenCode. State and write locks live under the repository's canonical Git common directory, `ralph/runs`, and are shared by linked worktrees. Independent repositories use independent stores. The plugin stays attached to the OpenCode server lifecycle; shutdown stops owned work and the dashboard. Restart reconciles facts and waits for explicit resume.

The v2 package ships server and TUI entry points. Slash commands execute directly, emit a status notification, and queue their exact output as a synthetic inbox item without waking the model. The TUI displays Ralph status notifications; the dashboard and persisted store remain authoritative.

The public `adapter`, `store`, `commands`, `types`, `review/*`, `reporting/completion` and `process` exports support typed host integration. `createCommandRouter(...).scheduleIssues(runId)` schedules the approved DAG; `executeIssue(runId, issueId)` executes/integrates one approved issue with at most three attempts and retained failure evidence; `answerExecutorQuestion(runId, issueId, response)` validates a host-provided correlated human response; `continuePlanning(runId)` presents and continues a pending execution approval; `runJob` dispatches a correlated session; `reviewFeature` coordinates the two reviewers and final report. `process` exposes ownership tracking/cleanup for approved command owners, not command approval. Tests and fixture code are excluded from runtime exports and the tarball.

Code and functional reviewers load their bundled `skills/code-review` and `skills/functional-review` resources. Code-review options accept a `{ providerID, modelID }` model; functional review accepts its own model through `functionalOptions`. Executor jobs carry the same optional model field. Model/tool availability and OpenCode permissions remain host-owned; no permission is auto-approved. Command configuration validates lifecycle ordering, platform coverage (or explicit unsupported reasons), paths, timeouts, argument arrays, explicit shells and environment references. Interview role settings are read from optional `.ralph/project.json`; other planning phases remain pending.

## Interview settings

Project replacements resolve relative to the workspace root. Omit `skillPaths.interview`
to use the original bundled grill-me guidance. Omit `models.interview` to capture the main
session's selected model; an unavailable selection produces an actionable error.

```json
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "models": { "interview": { "providerID": "your-provider", "modelID": "your-model" } },
  "skillPaths": { "interview": "skills/my-interview.md" },
  "concurrency": 2,
  "timeoutMs": 300000,
  "agentTimeoutMs": 1800000
}
```

Runs record model selections, skill paths and content hashes, the verified command hash,
and timeout/concurrency defaults. Agent timeouts interrupt planning and retain the lock
and evidence. Human answers do not time out. `agentTimeoutMs` separately controls executor and merger deadlines (default thirty minutes), and is recorded on the run and each issue attempt. One canonical repository lock includes
planning; explanation approval retains it for the next planning slice. Interrupted locks
require reconciliation; this slice does not release them or automatically restart planning.

## Concurrent execution resources

Ready issues are selected in approved graph order. A submission waits in the integration
queue; children start only after all parents integrate, including qualified integration
with omitted checks. `/ralph-status` and durable events expose ready, pending, running,
integration_queued, integrated, failed and blocked states plus queue order. The executor
slot ends after submission cleanup; merger/validation work uses a separate serial queue.

`/ralph-commands` may propose resource allocation in the exact approved configuration:

```json
{
  "resources": {
    "RALPH_PORT": "port",
    "RALPH_NAMESPACE": "namespace",
    "RALPH_DIRECTORY": "directory"
  },
  "exclusiveResources": ["shared-test-database"]
}
```

Command `env` mappings reference these source names, for example
`{"PORT":"RALPH_PORT","APP_NAMESPACE":"RALPH_NAMESPACE","APP_DATA":"RALPH_DIRECTORY"}`.
Ports, namespaces and directories are allocated per lifecycle/job. Executor prompts
receive the allocation; candidates receive fresh allocations. The runner executes only
approved commands, using main-session permission before an executor session exists.
Shared resource names serialize use within the repository. Startup without allocated
ports also serializes use. Missing required references block setup; commands are never
invented or rewritten to obtain isolation. Owned cleanup releases only that lifecycle's
resources; cleanup failures retain ownership and diagnostics.

## Executor questions

An executor may return `{"kind":"needs_input","question":"...","recommendedAnswer":"...","affectedRequirement":"..."}`. Ralph persists the question on its run/issue/attempt and publishes it through the main-session adapter. The issue and descendants wait while independent issues continue. Human waiting consumes no executor capacity, agent deadline or retry. A correlated clarification resumes the same worktree and attempt with a new job identity and the remaining active-time budget.

Hosts answer through the adapter's authenticated `UserInputResult` contract. The public router also accepts a later response via `answerExecutorQuestion`, followed by `scheduleIssues` for clarification or `continuePlanning` for revision approval. Stale requests, changed revisions, duplicate messages and unrelated sessions are rejected. The production adapter currently leaves human input pending because verified provenance is unavailable.

For scope changes, a correlated user answer can carry JSON `{"kind":"revise_scope","prd":"complete PRD text","graph":<complete graph>,"files":<issue-path to Markdown map>}`. Ralph compares content, validates and publishes a new draft, presents affected work and preserved integrated evidence, and requires the normal combined execution approval. Adapter-delivered answers present that gate automatically. Revised artifacts cannot execute under the previous approval. Answers and approvals have no browser mutation endpoint.

## Validation semantics

Three **total issue attempts** include the first submission and two retries; an expected failing TDD red test does not consume an attempt. Three **total review rounds** apply to the final dual review; each generated repair task has its own three-attempt limit. Functional infrastructure retries have a separate three-attempt budget. Issue repair limits are enforced through the public single-issue entrypoint; the dependency scheduler preserves independent progress when an issue exhausts its budget.

The intended validation policy skips only timed-out validation checks for the current run, never setup/readiness timeouts, permission denials, cancellations or completed failures. Those omissions must remain in the report and must not rewrite approved commands. The general timeout/skip executor and clean-checkout baseline are pending earlier slices; the demo supplies skipped evidence explicitly. A baseline must eventually pass on an isolated clean checkout before feature implementation. Retain failed worktrees and their evidence for diagnosis; clean successful worktrees only after validated integration. Recovery does not silently discard retained resources.

A completed agent job is not proof of a verified feature. Reports distinguish `verified`, `incomplete-validation` (including skipped checks), `unverified` (missing review evidence) and `unresolved` (blocking issues/findings). Inspect the report's commit, checks, unavailable review and cleanup/resource fields. No workflow script publishes, pushes, creates a PR or merges into a target branch.

## Reproduce the package demonstration

```sh
bun test tests/integration/15-package.test.ts
bun run test:package
bun test tests/e2e/full-workflow.test.ts
bun run test:browser
bun run test:smoke
```

The package probe builds a clean distribution, packs it, installs it into two committed temporary repositories with spaces in their paths, and loads the installed plugin. It checks bundled skills/dashboard assets, separate stores and dashboard data, concurrent ports, argv preservation, local readiness, child cleanup and pause/resume. The review demo commits a tiny addition feature, runs its test, seeds the absent execution handoff, and dispatches independent reviewer sessions through the fake SDK HTTP boundary. Reports are `verified` for the passing-evidence fixture and `incomplete-validation` for the skipped-evidence fixture. These are deterministic fixture verdicts, not live-agent evidence. Temporary repositories are removed; `test:package` prints platform, runtime, outcomes and blockers. Set `PACKAGE_EVIDENCE_PATH` to retain JSON evidence.

For the real installed OpenCode server probe, enable `OPENCODE_SMOKE=1` (PowerShell: `$env:OPENCODE_SMOKE='1'`) before `bun run test:smoke`. It uses a local deterministic model. The separate credentialed session probe requires `OPENCODE_LIVE_SMOKE=1`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_URL` and `OPENCODE_SMOKE_MODEL=provider/model`; without them it reports SKIPPED. Neither probe covers the absent setup-to-report workflow. A v2 server also requires authentication: set `OPENCODE_SMOKE_PASSWORD` for the live probe. Credentials remain in the configured server; do not copy them into fixtures or package assets.

See [troubleshooting](docs/troubleshooting.md). CI runs the required suites on macOS, Ubuntu and native Windows and retains per-platform package evidence. A green fixture demo does not remove the missing-phase or unrun-platform blockers.
# Installation and operational troubleshooting

Use the exact dependency versions listed in [compatibility](opencode-compatibility.md) and Bun from `.bun-version`. Run the package probe from the source checkout: `bun run test:package`.

| Symptom | Check or action |
| --- | --- |
| Tarball install mentions a developer's home directory | Rebuild the current manifest; it no longer contains a self-dependency. Copy the archive into the consumer and install `./opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz`. Avoid percent-encoded absolute tarball URLs with Bun 1.4.3. |
| Plugin fails to load on a path with spaces or Windows | Generate the canonical package-directory URL with `realpathSync` and `pathToFileURL` using the README command. Use the encoded URL for plugin loading; do not hand-build drive-letter file URLs. |
| Slash command missing | Restart the pinned OpenCode server in the correct repository and inspect `plugins` in its local `opencode.json`. V2 activates external plugins asynchronously; allow activation to finish. Only status/pause/resume/stop are implemented. Commands/orchestrator setup phases are pending. |
| Global OpenCode behaves differently | Use OpenCode 2.0.29. This package uses the v2 plugin contract; v1 is no longer supported. |
| Local test CLI says postinstall was not run | Run `bun pm trust @opencode/cli`; the manifest trusts only this pinned CLI install script. |
| HTTP SDK returns 401 | Pass the v2 server Basic-auth header to `createAdapterClient(url, fetch, headers)`. The smoke test uses an isolated test-only password. |
| Dashboard inaccessible | Get a fresh URL from `/ralph-status`. Use its loopback host and fragment token. Tokens are process-scoped and change on server restart. The listener stops when the plugin is disposed. |
| Dashboard returns 401/403/405 | Authentication is required for API reads. Cross-origin and mutation requests are rejected. Use OpenCode for controls. |
| Reopened workflow does not continue | Recovery intentionally waits for `/ralph-resume`. Read the recovery facts first; resolve blocked/conflicting Git, session or ownership evidence. Attempts and revision budgets do not reset. |
| Writer lock or process ownership cannot be inspected | Do not delete state or kill arbitrary PIDs. Recovery only reclaims provably dead local owners. Native Windows requires PowerShell/CIM and `taskkill.exe`; POSIX requires `ps` and process-group signals. Unknown ownership is a visible blocker. Network-filesystem lock atomicity is not promised. |
| Completion says incomplete/unverified | Read skipped checks, unavailable criteria/review, failed cleanup and retained resources in the report. A successful assistant response does not validate checks. |
| Failed worktree remains | Keep it with its evidence for diagnosis. Do not remove a worktree referenced by an active operation. Successful issue cleanup is owned by integration, not package installation. |
| Live smoke is skipped | The credentialed probe requires opt-in, endpoint, model and v2 server authentication documented in README. A deterministic local model or fake adapter cannot prove provider compatibility. |
| Linux or Windows result is unavailable | Run the corresponding native CI matrix job. Missing or failed supported-platform evidence remains a release blocker. macOS results cannot certify Windows cleanup behavior. |

No install or release script pushes, publishes, creates a PR or merges a feature into its target. The two-repository demonstration uses local temporary Git branches only. Runtime state is under the Git common directory; logs, repositories, test fixtures and secrets are excluded from the package's allowlisted contents. The build removes stale `dist` output before compilation.
import { continuePlanning } from "../core/orchestrator.js";
import {
  type InterviewOptions,
  startInterview,
} from "../planning/interview.js";
export async function orchestrateFeature(options: InterviewOptions) {
  const run = await startInterview(options);
  if (!run) return run;
  return continuePlanning(options.store, options.adapter, run.runId);
}
import type { Plugin } from "@opencode/plugin";
import type { CommandDefinition } from "@opencode/plugin/promise/command";
import type { Info } from "@opencode/plugin/promise/tool";
import { createAdapterClient } from "../../src/opencode/adapter.js";

export function createFakeOpenCode(options: { sessionPrefix?: string } = {}) {
  const requests: {
    path: string;
    directory: string | null;
    body: Record<string, unknown>;
  }[] = [];
  const sessions = new Map<string, { directory: string; text?: string }>();
  let denied = false,
    empty = false,
    deniedTool = false,
    invalidTool = false,
    holdCreation = false;
  let release: (() => void) | undefined;
  let gate: Promise<void> | undefined;
  let count = 0;
  let reply = "actual response";
  let failedCodeMode = false;
  const client = createAdapterClient(
    "http://fixture.invalid",
    async (request) => {
      const url = new URL(request.url);
      const raw = request.method === "POST" ? await request.text() : "";
      const body = raw ? (JSON.parse(raw) as Record<string, unknown>) : {};
      const sessionID = url.pathname.match(/\/session\/([^/]+)/)?.[1];
      const location = body.location as { directory?: string } | undefined;
      requests.push({
        path: url.pathname,
        directory:
          location?.directory ??
          (sessionID ? sessions.get(sessionID)?.directory : undefined) ??
          url.searchParams.get("location[directory]"),
        body,
      });
      if (denied)
        return Response.json(
          { _tag: "PermissionDeniedError", message: "Permission denied" },
          { status: 403 },
        );
      if (url.pathname.startsWith("/api/rpc/")) {
        const method = url.pathname.split("/").at(-1);
        return Response.json({
          output:
            method === "tools"
              ? [
                  "read",
                  "glob",
                  "grep",
                  "edit",
                  "write",
                  "shell",
                  "custom_tool",
                  "http",
                  "agent-browser",
                ]
              : { ok: true },
        });
      }
      if (url.pathname === "/api/session") {
        if (holdCreation) await gate;
        const id = `${options.sessionPrefix ?? "session"}-${++count}`;
        sessions.set(id, { directory: location?.directory ?? "" });
        return Response.json({
          data: {
            id,
            location: { directory: location?.directory },
            time: { created: 1, updated: 1 },
          },
        });
      }
      if (url.pathname.endsWith("/interrupt"))
        return Response.json({ data: { interrupted: true } });
      if (url.pathname.endsWith("/wait")) {
        await gate;
        return new Response(null, { status: 204 });
      }
      if (url.pathname.endsWith("/prompt")) {
        const session = sessions.get(sessionID as string);
        if (session) session.text = String(body.text);
        return Response.json({
          data: {
            id: "user-1",
            type: "user",
            sessionID,
            payload: { text: body.text },
            time: { created: 1 },
            delivery: "steer",
          },
        });
      }
      if (url.pathname.endsWith("/context")) {
        return Response.json({
          data: [
            {
              id: "user-1",
              type: "user",
              text: sessions.get(sessionID as string)?.text,
              time: { created: 1 },
            },
            {
              id: "earlier-message",
              type: "assistant",
              time: { created: 1, completed: 2 },
              content: invalidTool
                ? [
                    {
                      type: "tool",
                      name: "invalid",
                      state: {
                        status: "completed",
                        input: {},
                        content: [{ type: "text", text: "unavailable" }],
                      },
                    },
                  ]
                : failedCodeMode
                  ? [
                      {
                        type: "tool",
                        name: "execute",
                        state: {
                          status: "completed",
                          input: {},
                          content: [
                            { type: "text", text: "caught inner failure" },
                          ],
                          metadata: {
                            toolCalls: [{ tool: "shell", status: "error" }],
                          },
                        },
                      },
                    ]
                  : deniedTool
                    ? [
                        {
                          type: "tool",
                          name: "shell",
                          state: {
                            status: "error",
                            error: {
                              type: "PermissionDenied",
                              message:
                                "The user rejected permission to use this tool",
                            },
                          },
                        },
                      ]
                    : [],
            },
            {
              id: "message-1",
              type: "assistant",
              time: { created: 1, completed: 2 },
              finish: "stop",
              content: [{ type: "text", text: empty ? "" : reply }],
            },
            {
              id: "idle-1",
              type: "idle",
              outcome: "succeeded",
              time: { created: 3 },
            },
          ],
        });
      }
      if (url.pathname.endsWith("/synthetic"))
        return Response.json({
          data: {
            id: "synthetic-1",
            sessionID,
            type: "synthetic",
            payload: body,
            time: { created: 1 },
            delivery: "steer",
          },
        });
      return Response.json({ data: true });
    },
  );
  return {
    client,
    requests,
    respond: (text: string) => {
      reply = text;
    },
    denyTool: () => {
      deniedTool = true;
    },
    invalidTool: () => {
      invalidTool = true;
    },
    failCodeMode: () => {
      failedCodeMode = true;
    },
    empty: () => {
      empty = true;
    },
    holdCreation: () => {
      holdCreation = true;
      gate = new Promise<void>((r) => {
        release = r;
      });
    },
    deny: () => {
      denied = true;
    },
    hold: () => {
      gate = new Promise<void>((r) => {
        release = r;
      });
    },
    release: () => release?.(),
  };
}

// Exercises the actual v2 registration and cleanup contracts without a model.
export function createFakePluginContext(
  directory: string,
  client = createFakeOpenCode().client,
) {
  const commands = new Map<string, CommandDefinition>();
  const tools = new Map<string, Info & { id: string }>();
  const hooks = new Map<string, (input: unknown) => Promise<void> | void>();
  const statuses: string[] = [];
  const rpcHandlers = new Map<string, (input: never) => Promise<unknown>>();
  const registration = (dispose = async () => {}) => ({ dispose });
  const context = {
    location: { directory },
    session: {
      ...client.session,
      hook: async (
        name: string,
        hook: (input: unknown) => Promise<void> | void,
      ) => {
        hooks.set(`session.${name}`, hook);
        return registration(async () => {
          hooks.delete(`session.${name}`);
        });
      },
    },
    command: {
      transform: async (
        apply: (editor: { add(definition: CommandDefinition): void }) => void,
      ) => {
        apply({
          add: (definition) => {
            commands.set(definition.name, definition);
          },
        });
        return registration(async () => {
          commands.clear();
        });
      },
    },
    tool: {
      list: async () => [...tools.values()],
      reload: async () => {},
      transform: async (apply: (editor: { add(info: Info): void }) => void) => {
        const added: string[] = [];
        apply({
          add: (info) => {
            tools.set(info.name, { ...info, id: info.name });
            added.push(info.name);
          },
        });
        return registration(async () => {
          for (const name of added) tools.delete(name);
        });
      },
      hook: async (
        name: string,
        hook: (input: unknown) => Promise<void> | void,
      ) => {
        hooks.set(`tool.${name}`, hook);
        return registration(async () => {
          hooks.delete(`tool.${name}`);
        });
      },
    },
    rpc: {
      register: async (
        _definition: unknown,
        handlers: Record<string, (input: never) => Promise<unknown>>,
      ) => {
        for (const [name, handler] of Object.entries(handlers))
          rpcHandlers.set(name, handler);
        return {
          ...registration(async () => {
            rpcHandlers.clear();
          }),
          events: {
            emit: async (_name: string, data: { text: string }) => {
              statuses.push(data.text);
            },
          },
        };
      },
    },
    event: {
      subscribe: async function* (_options: { signal?: AbortSignal }) {},
    },
  } as unknown as Plugin.Context;
  return { context, commands, tools, hooks, statuses, rpcHandlers };
}

