# Unverified boundaries

- Linux and Windows probes are configured in CI but have not run locally. Version pins reflect the macOS probe, not a proven cross-platform minimum.
- V2 has session forms, but Ralph has no verified human-input/approval provenance workflow. `requestUserInput` returns `unsupported`; user-message role alone cannot establish human approval provenance. The command setup state machine accepts correlated host-supplied human approval, but production setup remains blocked until an explicit provenance channel is verified.
- Ralph RPC notifications are location-scoped; the TUI subscription filters its location. Direct commands queue exact output as synthetic inbox items without waking the model. Interactive rendering still needs native validation.
- Writer locks now validate recorded PID birth identity before replacing a dead owner. Missing/corrupt ownership and unavailable inspection remain contention errors; network-filesystem semantics remain unverified.
- Credentialed live-provider smoke is skipped unless explicitly configured. Dashboard Chromium checks now run on macOS; Linux/Windows browser behavior remains unverified locally.

- Issue 8 execution scheduling is not present. The dashboard exposes persisted `recordExecution` evidence but automatic graph/queue/check publication must be wired by that predecessor. Intermediate logs also require a persisted source beyond the current terminal job output.

- Issue 11 now starts code review after issue 8 integrates the complete graph, using issue 4's hash-bound approved requirements. Functional review/repair lifecycle wiring remains with issues 12/13. Review refs/worktrees remain retained, and interrupted review operations await issue 14 reconciliation. Read-only tool selection uses the v2 Ralph catalog RPC and permission-preserving observation aliases; local-model linked-worktree restrictions are exercised by the real smoke probe. Live-provider enforcement remains unverified.
- Issue 12's public review boundary needs issue 8 to supply the approved environment
  lifecycle and actual feature surfaces; the command integration must register or
  supply dedicated OpenCode browser/HTTP observation tools. Missing tools explicitly
  exhaust three attempts as unverified. Real agent-browser plus HTTP fixture checks
  passed on macOS using fake reviewer output; live-provider and Linux/Windows checks
  remain unverified. Checklist extraction currently expects explicit acceptance lists
  and graph issue `acceptanceCriteria`; authoritative artifact schema integration is pending.
- Issue 13 now defaults approved repair runs to the existing executor/merger/check pipeline and releases matching terminal active-run locks. Host-provided functional environment/surface/tool options are still required for `reviewFeature`; the scheduler’s automatic handoff currently starts only code review. Clarification uses the originating role plus persisted context in a new read-only session. Live-provider enforcement remains unverified.
- Issue 14 now reconciles production integrations, worktree creation, accepted refs and active-run lock identity. Explicit resume republishes pending questions with fresh identities; answers, approvals and budgets survive fixture restarts. Ambiguous execution/repair/publication/cleanup stays blocked. Host-supplied functional review options are not reconstructed automatically; production human provenance, native Linux/Windows process trees and live shutdown remain unverified. See [recovery](features/workflow-recovery.md).

- Issue 15 installs the real tarball in two consumers and probes installed process ownership, reviewer skills, dashboard and report semantics. Complete setup-to-report remains explicitly skipped until slices 2–9 exist; independent project setup configuration and the production active-run lock still need that integration. Platform matrix jobs are configured, not executed by local macOS evidence. See [package distribution](features/package-distribution.md).

